  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nTemperatures will not rebound much from the morning lows as highs \ntoday will only be in the 40s. Strong low-level flow will linger \nover the region through most of the day and mix down to the surface, \nresulting in wind gusts over 20 mph at times. These gusty winds will \nmake it feel quite blustery as wind chills will be in the 20s this \nmorning and likely stay in the 30s for most of the region today. The \ncold northwesterly flow will also support some lake effect showers \nthrough the morning and possibly into the early afternoon. Some wet \nsnow may mix in early this morning but little to no accumulation is \nexpected. High pressure will begin to build into the region later \ntoday and will help cut off any lingering showers as it brings a \ndrier airmass. The northwesterly flow will keep skies cloudy into \nthe afternoon, though skies will eventually clear to full sunshine.\n\nThe first hard freeze of the season is expected tonight into Sunday \nmorning. Temperatures were lowered slightly below NBM guidance given \nthat conditions will be favorable for good radiational cooling. Low \ntemperatures will be in the 20s across the region. The Freeze Watch \nfor Luzerne and Lackawanna Counties has been upgrade to a Freeze \nWarning. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe high pressure will stay over the region and most of the eastern \nUS throughout next week. A low pressure system will develop along \nthe East Coast Sunday and lingers off the Carolinas and mid-Atlantic \nthrough most of the work week. The high pressure will keep \nprecipitation associated with the low out of the region. Gradual \nwarm air advection after tonight will help temperatures get back \ncloser to seasonable normals by Monday. Temperatures will vary \nlittle after Sunday with highs consistently in the 60s and lows in \nthe 30s and 40s. Some valley locations will approach the 70s for \ndaytime highs during the midweek period.",
